course: dictionary
lesson: 765
title: Beaks and food
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A beak is the hard, pointed mouth of a bird. Birds use it to eat, pick things up and build nests. Some other animals, like a turtle or an octopus, also have a hard mouth like this.
explain: The shape of a beak tells you what a bird eats. «Eagles are big birds of prey: hunters with hooked beaks, huge feet and amazing eyes.» The hooked shape helps an eagle grip and tear its food.
explain: Look at the words around beak to find out what kind of animal is meant. In stories, a bird may peck or tap with its beak, so the words «peck with its beak» are a clue that the animal is a bird.
fact: A beak is the hard, pointed mouth of a bird.
fact: «A hooked beak» tears meat, «a long thin beak» reaches nectar, and «a short thick beak» cracks seeds.
fact: «Darwin's finches have beaks suited to seeds, insects or cactus.» Their beaks are different shapes because they eat different food.
fact: «Ostriches ... turn their eggs in the nest with their beaks.»
fact: Turtles and octopuses have a hard mouth that is similar to a bird's beak.
exercise: What is a beak? | A kind of nest | A type of wing feather | *A hard, pointed mouth of a bird | A soft, bendy tail
exercise: In science, a bird with a long thin beak can reach what? | *Nectar | Seeds | Meat | Stones
exercise: «Ostriches ... turn their eggs in the nest with their beaks.» Which body part do ostriches use to turn their eggs? | Tail | *Beak | Wings | Feet
test: Which bird's beak cracks palm nuts so hard that people need a hammer? | Darwin's finch | *Hyacinth macaw | Ostrich | Eagle
test: Which animal has a hard mouth that is similar to a bird's beak? | *Octopus | Horse | Rabbit | Dog
exam: Why do Darwin's finches have beaks of different shapes? | Beaks grow longer when it is cold | *Birds whose beaks fitted their food survived better, so beaks changed over generations | Each finch chose its own beak shape | They were painted different colours
